    id: "f234f5e65644d35d097b",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Takaichi vows to attend deliberations on tax-cut bill ‘in good faith’",
    summary: "Takaichi made these remarks at a news conference in Shenzhen, China, following a series of diplomatic engagements in the Philippines and China.",
    preview: "Shenzhen – Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi said Thursday that she will attend parliamentary deliberations on the consumption tax cut bill “in good faith” and will make every effort for its early passage. Takaichi made these remarks at a news conference in Shenzhen, southern China, following a series of diplomatic engagements in the Philippines and China. “I advanced Japan’s strong diplomacy and gained solid achievements,” she said.",
    body: "Shenzhen – Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi said Thursday that she will attend parliamentary deliberations on the consumption tax cut bill “in good faith” and will make every effort for its early passage.\n\nTakaichi made these remarks at a news conference in Shenzhen, southern China, following a series of diplomatic engagements in the Philippines and China. “I advanced Japan’s strong diplomacy and gained solid achievements,” she said.\n\nRegarding the planned benefit program included in the tax cut bill, the prime minister reiterated that the government is considering offering benefits to at least 50% of workers, based on calls from opposition parties.\n\n“We’ll attend deliberations in good faith to gain support from as many parties as possible and begin preparations for its full implementation as soon as possible,” she said.\n\nThe prime minister did not discuss the possibility of enacting the tax cut bill with a two-thirds majority in the House of Representatives if the bill is rejected by the House of Councilors, where the ruling parties lack a majority.\n\nAhead of the revision of Japan’s three key national security documents later this year, Takaichi said that no decisions have been made regarding the size of defense spending for the five years from fiscal 2027 and funding plans. “We’ll promote discussions while considering fiscal sustainability,” she said.",
