    id: "b3b10d3206e1403ad6af",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Japan government under fire for using conspiracy theory YouTuber in PR",
    summary: "The government says that the use of the YouTuber is intended to deepen understanding of security policy among people in their 20s and 30s.",
    preview: "The government has come under fire for featuring a YouTuber known for spreading conspiracy theories and urban legends on an official public relations website aimed at promoting understanding of its security policies. The government says that the use of the YouTuber is intended to deepen understanding of security policy among people in their 20s and 30s. However, opposition parties and others criticize the move, saying it undermines the credibility of government communications.",
    body: "The government has come under fire for featuring a YouTuber known for spreading conspiracy theories and urban legends on an official public relations website aimed at promoting understanding of its security policies.\n\nThe government says that the use of the YouTuber is intended to deepen understanding of security policy among people in their 20s and 30s. However, opposition parties and others criticize the move, saying it undermines the credibility of government communications.\n\nThe government’s Public Relations Office website launched a special feature page on Monday titled “Kore Wa Toshi Densetsu Dewa Nai” (“This is not an Urban Legend“), explaining how national security is connected to everyday life.\n\nThe YouTuber featured on the page, Naokiman, has previously spread unsubstantiated claims, including that a major disaster would strike in July 2025 and that Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi is a descendant of Emperor Tenmu, a Japanese emperor of the seventh century.\n\nIn a video posted on his YouTube channel, Naokiman explained that he received a job offer from the Cabinet Office and said, “What comes out of my mouth is no longer an urban legend; it’s become official.”\n\nAt a news conference Wednesday, Chief Cabinet Secretary Minoru Kihara said the Cabinet Office selected a proposal from a contractor that won the project through a competitive bidding process.\n\nYuichiro Tamaki, leader of the opposition Democratic Party for the People, questioned the government’s decision, saying it “undermines the credibility of government communications and gives official backing to information that is not based on facts.”\n\nThe move has also drawn criticism on social media, with some calling the page “propaganda funded by taxpayers.”\n\nLast month, the Public Relations Office published a feature page explaining the importance of national security in the style of a husband-and-wife comedy duo. The page was designed to target women aged 40 and older.\n\nA Cabinet Office official said each of the two projects had a budget of ¥150 million.\n\nRyosuke Nishida, a professor of sociology at Nihon University in Tokyo, described the initiatives as “part of a public relations strategy adapted to the changing times.”\n\nHowever, he added it is “necessary to consider whether it is appropriate for government public relations to use content creators who disseminate information mixing fact and fiction or exploit viewers’ anxieties and curiosity.”",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "The Japan Times",
    url: "https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2026/11/19/japan/conspiracy-theory-youtuber-pr",
    time: "2 ore fa",
    pub_ts: 1795085520,
    published_at: "2026-11-19T10:52:00+00:00",
    language: "en",
    score: 1,
