    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Badenoch says using amputee athletes to justify cutting disability benefits was ‘message of hope’ – UK politics live",
    summary: "PM has asked Badenoch to withdraw her suggestion that benefits should be cut because ‘double amputees run marathons’ These are from my colleague Peter Walker , who was at the Tory event where James Cleverly was named as the party’s candidate for London mayor. Peter posted this before the result was announced. Khan (who may or may not stand a 4th time) has thus far faced a dilettante who decided to campaign on a racist platform (Zack Goldsmith) and two oddballs in Shaun Bailey and then Susan Hall. Not even he can be lucky yet again Interesting that [the Conservatives] did not say how many votes were cast, just the percentage - which perhaps indicates it was not a huge number Cleverly’s speech is mainly an attack on Labour and Khan, which is likely to be a theme for his campaign: “Burnham can’t stand London and Khan stand up to Burnham.” Also a pitch about building houses and cutting crime, and boosting transport. Cleverly says he would “end Khan’s war on motorists”, which is a sign he might lean into the anti-cycling and walking rhetoric which the London Tories have a habit of espousing. Expect talk about LTNs and 20mph zones. In the media Q&A Cleverly is pinned down on whether he backs the Tory party line on wanting to leave the ECHR and opposes net zero. He confirms he does (as he has to). Such things will be ready ammunition for Labour. Cleverly says of Andy Burnham, “His residual behaviour is to be anti-London”. This will very much be a theme of his campaign, it seems. Not sure how resonant a “poor London!” message will be even in London People are leaving London, or not choosing to come here in the first place, businesses, job creators, families leaving London, and by the time of the election in 2028, Khan will have been mayor for 12 years, and those two things are related. The problems London faces are the symptoms. Khan is the cause. Continue reading...",
    preview: "PM has asked Badenoch to withdraw her suggestion that benefits should be cut because ‘double amputees run marathons’ These are from my colleague Peter Walker , who was at the Tory event where James Cleverly was named as the party’s candidate for London mayor. Peter posted this before the result was announced. Khan (who may or may not stand a 4th time) has thus far faced a dilettante who decided to campaign on a racist platform (Zack Goldsmith) and two oddballs in Shaun Bailey and then Susan Hall.",
    body: "Badenoch says using amputee athletes to justify cutting disability benefits was ‘message of hope’\n\nGood morning. At PMQs yesterday Andy Burnham said that Kemi Badenoch should withdraw the “double amputees run marathons” comment that she used to justify the Tory plan to cut sickness and disability benefits by £14bn. It was “crass and insensitive”, he said.\n\nThis morning Badenoch defended what she was saying claimed that, in fact, she was delivering a “message of hope”.\n\nIn an interview on the Today programme, asked if she accepted that her remark was insensitive, Badenoch said she didn’t. She went on:\n\nI will speak the truth and I will say what I believe is common sense. And what I know is that, if people can work, then we should encourage them to work.\n\nAnd what I’m saying is actually a message of hope, that if people even with significant, problems can work, then those who have ADHD and mild anxiety should go to work.\n\nWhen the presenter, Nick Robinson, put it to her that it was unreasonable to use an exceptional case as a guide to what most disabled people should be doing, Badenoch did not agree. “We do use extreme examples to illustrate things,” she said.\n\nRobinson tried again, saying she was taking “one extreme case [to] imply that anybody on disability benefits should just get on with it”. Badenoch accused him of “faux outrage” and “tone policing”. She added:\n\nPeople are free to disagree, but they know where I’m coming from.\n\nI will post more from the interview shortly.\n\nToday the news will be dominated by two summits. This afternoon Burnham is in Berlin for a meeting of the pro-Ukraine coalition of the willing. We have coverage of this on our Europe live blog.\n\nAnd in China, Vladimir Putin, who invaded Ukraine, Xi Jinping, his leading ally, and Donald Trump, who is seen by many as a closet Putin ally, are meeting now. You can read about that here.\n\nWe will have comments open in about an hour. That is because I will be doing a Q&A this afternoon, probably from about 3pm, and I will try to answer as many questions as possible. Do post them below the line.\n\nHere is the agenda for the day.\n\n9.30am: Louise Haigh, the first secretary of state and Cabinet Office minister, takes questions in the Commons.\n\n10am: The Conservative party announces the results of the first round of voting in the election of its candidate for London mayor for 2028. James Cleverly, the former home secretary and former foreign secretary, is expected to get more than 50% of the vote, meaning a second round of voting should not be necessary.\n\n11.30am: Downing Street holds a lobby briefing.\n\nNoon: Heather Hallett, the Covid inquiry chair, publishes her report on the test, trace and isolate system.\n\nAround 12.15pm (UK time): Andy Burnham arrives in Berlin for a meeting of the pro-Ukraine coalition of the willing. He is expected to speak to the media later in the afternoon after the plenary has finished.\n\n1pm: Chris Bryant, the Northern Ireland secretary, attends a meeting of the British-Irish Intergovernmental Conference in Dublin.",
